# Fixed defect 133's second round, 2026-09-29. Symptom: every swap was
# `certain` wherever its word stood, so `check --apply` wrote `include
# "stdio.h"` into `use "stdio.h"`, which then read `stdio.h.hero`; `enum` in
# an `extern` group into a `variant` no group holds; `switch` at the top of
# a file into a `match` that declares nothing; `int` opening a line into
# `i64 count`; `fn` and `struct` inside a body into declarations that cannot
# stand there; `switch` with no value and `elif` with no `if` above it into
# a `match` and an `else if` the parser refuses. Each was a program `check`
# refused with a code the first run had not reported. Cause: defect 015's
# rule read one byte, the one after the word; where the word stands was
# read by nobody. A swap is now certain only where the word it writes is
# right in that place (`foreign_at.hero`, from the text in hand,
# `word_place.hero`), and elsewhere the message says what is right there.
# Measured over the twelve words with a swap in 61 places each, 732
# programs, `check`, `check --apply`, `check`: a certain swap left a code
# of the parse the first run had not reported in 514 on the trunk's
# compiler and in one here, `elif x == 2:`, whose colon waits because the
# parser does not read the `elif` line (grammar_expr's, given to lane 132).
# Eleven more show a later stage's code, the programs' own, reached once
# the parse is clean. Nothing below is certain, so `--apply` writes the
# file back unchanged.

include "stdio.h"  #~ reserved_word

switch Mode  #~ reserved_word

int count  #~ reserved_word

extern "m.h"
    enum Mode  #~ reserved_word
        a
    function q() -> i64

function area(p: struct) -> i64  #~ reserved_word
    return 1

function main()
    x = 1
    fn inner()  #~ reserved_word
    struct Point  #~ reserved_word
    import geom  #~ reserved_word
    y = switch  #~ reserved_word
    elif x == 2  #~ reserved_word
    print(x + y + area(p: 1))
